### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer is testing accountability, honesty, and recovery. They want to see that you take ownership, communicate bad news early, and turn a miss into a lasting process improvement.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class DeadlineTest {
    enum Signal { ACCOUNTABLE, EVASIVE }

    public static Signal evaluate(boolean ownedIt, boolean communicatedEarly, boolean improvedProcess) {
        return (ownedIt && communicatedEarly && improvedProcess) ? Signal.ACCOUNTABLE : Signal.EVASIVE;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true, true));   // ACCOUNTABLE
    }
}
```

### How do you detect slippage early?

Track remaining work against an ideal burn-down line every day. If by day three of a two-week sprint you are behind the line, raise it immediately.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class BurnDownTracker {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(BurnDownTracker.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int totalPoints = 40;
        int day = 3;
        int idealRemaining = totalPoints - (totalPoints * day / 10); // 10-day sprint
        int actualRemaining = 35;
        log.info(() -> "Day " + day + ": ideal=" + idealRemaining + " actual=" + actualRemaining);
        if (actualRemaining > idealRemaining) {
            log.warning("SLIPPAGE DETECTED: raise it now, not at deadline");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Day 3: ideal=28 actual=35
SLIPPAGE DETECTED: raise it now, not at deadline
```

### How do you communicate the slippage?

Go to your manager and stakeholders with the exact evidence, the real root cause, and a revised plan. Do not blame any individual.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class SlippageCommunication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("To manager and stakeholders:");
        System.out.println("  Evidence: burn-down shows 35 points remaining vs 28 ideal");
        System.out.println("  Root cause: audit service dependency is more complex than estimated");
        System.out.println("  Not blaming: the estimate was simply wrong, not anyone's fault");
        System.out.println("  Revised plan: ship core search on time, defer advanced filters");
    }
}
```

### How do you re-scope intelligently?

Rather than ship everything late or ship something half-tested on time, deliver the critical path on the original date and defer nice-to-haves.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class IntelligentRescope {
    record Feature(String name, String priority, String delivery) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Feature> plan = List.of(
            new Feature("Transaction search (core)",     "P0", "On time"),
            new Feature("Date range filter",             "P0", "On time"),
            new Feature("Advanced multi-account filter", "P1", "Deferred 1 week"),
            new Feature("Export to PDF",                 "P2", "Deferred 1 week")
        );
        plan.forEach(f -> System.out.println(f.name() + " [" + f.priority() + "] -> " + f.delivery()));
    }
}
```

### Why should you protect quality under deadline pressure?

In banking, a rushed money-handling bug is far more expensive than a one-week delay. Quality on the critical path is non-negotiable.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class QualityOverSpeed {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Cost of 1-week delay: minor stakeholder disappointment");
        System.out.println("Cost of money bug in production: customer impact + regulatory risk");
        System.out.println("Decision: protect quality on money paths, always");
    }
}
```

### How do you improve estimation for next time?

Add a spike task to de-risk unknowns before committing to a date. Track estimate vs actual to calibrate over time.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class EstimationImprovement {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("New process:");
        System.out.println("  1. Spike task: explore unknown dependencies before committing");
        System.out.println("  2. Track estimate vs actual for every task");
        System.out.println("  3. Derive calibration factor from history");
        System.out.println("  Result: estimates became far more reliable");
    }
}
```

### How do you take ownership without blaming others?

Use phrases like "the estimate was wrong" instead of "Amit's estimate was wrong." Own the delivery outcome as a team, and own your part specifically.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class OwnershipLanguage {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Blame: 'Amit underestimated the audit dependency'");
        System.out.println("Ownership: 'We underestimated the audit dependency; "
            + "I should have added a spike to de-risk it'");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "Would you commit to the same timeline again?" (no, I would add a spike), "How did stakeholders react?" (they appreciated early honesty), and "What if you could not re-scope?" (negotiate timeline, never quality).

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class DeadlineFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Same timeline? -> No, I would add a spike first");
        System.out.println("Stakeholder reaction? -> Appreciated early honesty");
        System.out.println("Cannot re-scope? -> Negotiate timeline, never ship broken code");
    }
}
```
